%T A399757 17227030541046,580043186778282
%N A399757 Largest integer k such that {1, 2, ..., k} can be partitioned into two subsets, with no two distinct elements of the same subset summing to a perfect n-th power.
%C A399757 The n-th powers u^n split the numbers into bands between consecutive powers. The decisive band is the one corresponding to p, the least integer with p^n < 2(p-1)^n; above p^n/2 every x has two smaller partners p^n-x and (p-1)^n-x, so this is the first band where the two reductions can close into cycles. We therefore do not scan points one by one: each band is represented as a finite system of interval relations, and the highest band is peeled by composing its internal relations and folding the resulting constraints down to the next lower band. Repeating this interval-level peeling produces a small residual system, whose satisfiability gives the feasible side, while an odd cycle in the residual system is lifted band by band back to the original system to prove infeasibility.
%H A399757 Zhihu, <a href="https://www.zhihu.com/question/1897328917376909724/answer/2079117465620058924">Explanation and computation details of the search</a> (in Chinese).
%e A399757 a(2) = 14. A valid partition of {1,...,14} into two parts with no part containing two numbers whose sum is a square is {1, 2, 4, 6, 9, 11, 13} and {3, 5, 7, 8, 10, 12, 14}. All pair-sums inside one part avoid 4, 9, 16 and 25. N = 15 is impossible: 15+10=5^2, 10+6=4^2, 6+3=3^2, 3+1=2^2, 1+15=4^2 forms a loop with odd length.
%Y A399757 Cf. A000290, A000578.
